== SAM Prompt Sensitivity and Learned Prompt Placement

SAM's segmentation quality is demonstrably sensitive to the spatial location of input prompts. Bui et al. @bui2024samrobust conducted a systematic robustness study showing that segmentation accuracy varies dramatically as a function of prompt placement. Points placed near object centroids yield the highest IoU, while those near object boundaries produce substantially degraded masks. This finding motivates the central premise of our work. If prompt location matters this much, then _learning_ where to place prompts should yield meaningful gains over heuristic or random placement. Several recent methods attempt to address this sensitivity through feature-matching heuristics. PerSAM @zhang2024persam achieves training-free one-shot personalization by computing cosine similarity between a reference image's features and the target, using the highest-similarity location as a point prompt. Matcher @liu2024matcher similarly combines DINOv2 @oquab2024dinov2 feature correspondences with SAM in a training-free pipeline for one-shot segmentation. SAM-PT @rajic2023sampt extends prompt derivation to video by propagating point prompts across frames via point tracking. While these approaches produce reasonable prompts without any training, they remain fundamentally _non-adaptive_, relying on fixed similarity metrics that cannot incorporate segmentation feedback or correct poor initial placements.

Supervised approaches offer an alternative path. RSPrompter @chen2024rsprompter learns a prompt generator for remote sensing imagery that keeps SAM frozen while training a lightweight module to produce task-specific prompts in a single forward pass. This represents progress over heuristic methods but remains limited to single-pass generation without iterative refinement. The broader prompt-learning literature reinforces the value of learned over hand-crafted prompts. Visual Prompt Tuning @jia2022vpt demonstrated that inserting a small number of learnable tokens into frozen Vision Transformers, updating less than one percent of parameters, matches or exceeds full fine-tuning on many benchmarks. In the language-vision domain, CoOp @zhou2022coop replaced hand-designed CLIP text prompts with learned continuous context vectors, achieving substantial gains in few-shot recognition. CoCoOp @zhou2022cocoop further improved on this by generating _input-conditional_ prompts through a lightweight Meta-Net, showing that image-specific prompt generation outperforms static learned prompts across distribution shifts.

Taken together, these works establish two key findings. First, prompt quality has an outsized effect on downstream performance, whether in segmentation, classification, or vision-language tasks. Second, the progression from hand-crafted to learned to input-conditional prompts consistently improves results. Our work extends this trajectory into a new regime. Rather than learning a static prompt or a single-pass generator, we train an RL policy that _iteratively_ selects prompts conditioned on the evolving segmentation state, combining the adaptiveness missing from heuristic methods with the reward-driven optimization absent from supervised approaches.
